From: Kenneth Kasilag <kenneth@kasilag.me>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 13:34:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: route and inject virtual port ingress

The downlink offload - a wired-to-wireless flow forwarded to a virtual port
- leaves the port's ingress L3 interface unbound, because only that
direction crosses the hardware. Bind it: a virtual port now takes the same
port-to-L3-interface binding as a physical port, so a packet that enters
the PPE with the virtual port as its source is routed and NAT'd by the flow
engine like any ingress. It reuses the L3 interface the downlink already
programs with the router MAC, which is the one the reverse (wireless-to-
wired) flow of the connection resolves.

Offload the wireless-to-wired direction. A routed flow's downlink is
already hardware-forwarded to a virtual port; its uplink still runs on the
netfilter software fast path because packets the wireless radio receives
never traverse the PPE. Add the primitive that puts them there:
edma_tx_vp_inject() submits a received frame on a Tx descriptor ring tagged
with the virtual port as its SOURCE, so the PPE runs the L3 ingress
pipeline - flow lookup, NAT, forward-to-WAN - in hardware.

Merged from:
  0519-01-net-ethernet-qualcomm-ppe-route-virtual-port-ingress.patch
  0519-02-net-ethernet-qualcomm-ppe-inject-virtual-port-uplink.patch

